{:receipt :kotobase/cold-isolate-rate
 :date "2026-08-18"
 :measured-at "2026-08-18T08:43Z/08:46Z"
 :graph "kotobase-protocols-v2"
 :worker "kotobase-protocols-worker"
 :deployed-version "pre-h8 (the build in production carries no L2 tier)"

 :question
 "How often does a HYDRATING request find the isolate block cache empty?

  Not the same question as `has this isolate ever hydrated`, which is what
  twelve bare /_diag/hydrate reads answered on the same day (twelve times: no).
  A bare diagnostic read does not hydrate, so it can only report on isolates
  that happened to have done so earlier. The cost of a served read depends on
  the other question, and it had not been asked."

 :method
 "Ten paired requests, each pair on ONE connection via `curl --next` and each
  pair on a FRESH connection:
    1. GET atproto.kotobase.net/xrpc/com.atproto.repo.describeRepo?repo=x
       -- 501 MethodNotImplemented, and that is expected: the shared-graph
       hydrate runs in the router BEFORE nsid dispatch, so the request performs
       the full hydrate and then declines the method. Credential-free.
    2. GET /_diag/hydrate on the same connection, so the diagnostic reports the
       isolate the probe actually used.
  Separate connections land on different isolates; that is the point here
  rather than a hazard, because the distribution IS the measurement."

 :samples
 [{:wall-s 3.82 :gets 84 :from-cache 0  :from-r2 84 :isolate :cold}
  {:wall-s 3.54 :gets 84 :from-cache 0  :from-r2 84 :isolate :cold}
  {:wall-s 4.30 :gets 84 :from-cache 0  :from-r2 84 :isolate :cold}
  {:wall-s 2.47 :gets 84 :from-cache 84 :from-r2 0  :isolate :warm}
  {:wall-s 3.14 :gets 84 :from-cache 0  :from-r2 84 :isolate :cold}
  {:wall-s 2.24 :gets 84 :from-cache 84 :from-r2 0  :isolate :warm}
  {:wall-s 4.92 :gets 84 :from-cache 0  :from-r2 84 :isolate :cold}
  {:wall-s 2.29 :gets 84 :from-cache 84 :from-r2 0  :isolate :warm}
  {:wall-s 3.11 :gets 84 :from-cache 84 :from-r2 0  :isolate :warm}
  {:wall-s 3.09 :gets 84 :from-cache 0  :from-r2 84 :isolate :cold}]

 :result
 {:cold 6 :warm 4 :n 10 :cold-rate 0.6
  :cold-wall-median-s 3.34 :warm-wall-median-s 2.38
  :class-b-ops-per-served-read 50.4
  :every-sample-touched-84-blocks true
  :note "The block COUNT never varies -- 84 distinct blocks, 11,462,579 bytes,
         63 unfolded transactions, every time. What varies is who answers."}

 :what-this-overturned
 {:premise "L1 is isolate memory and every measured request lands on a cold
            isolate, so the 84 gets in the baseline are unavoidable."
  :status :false-as-stated
  :measured "4 requests in 10 were answered entirely from L1."
  :consequence
  "The cost model's `:class-b-ops 84` came from ONE sample and is the COLD
   case. The measured mix is 50.4 gets per served read, so the class-B term was
   overstated by 40%, and every hypothesis that removes gets was credited with
   removing more than exist. h8-l2-cache-api fell from $22.68 to $13.61 and
   from first in the batch to fourth in the ranking."}

 :the-confound-and-its-direction
 "Ten requests in ~35 seconds warm the isolates the next request may land on,
  so 0.6 is an UPPER bound on coldness under back-to-back traffic. This graph
  has no organic traffic (twelve isolates that had never hydrated, same day), so
  its real regime is nearer 1.0. Both bounds are recorded; the loop carries 0.6
  as a parameter rather than folding it into the numbers, because the two
  regimes give different answers and it should be possible to see which one was
  priced."

 :what-it-did-not-change
 "The CPU term, and therefore h7. A warm request still costs 2.38 s of wall
  against 3.34 s cold -- the cache removes the round trips and leaves the parse
  standing, which is the same split the phase bench found from the inside.
  ~1.0 s of a cold request is the 84 gets; the rest is CPU that no cache tier
  can reach."

 :not-in-the-judge
 "Wall clock. Cloudflare bills CPU, not elapsed time, so the ~1.0 s the gets
  cost a user does not appear in cost-per-million at all. A latency argument
  and a cost argument can therefore disagree about a cache tier, and this one
  is worth more to a user than to the bill."}
